    "source": "-- Proofs, Checked — Chapter 2: famous fallacies.\n-- INTENDED TO FAIL. Each \"theorem\" below is a classic invalid argument.\n-- Cetacea rejects all three; the chapter walks through the error\n-- messages, including the countermodel notes.\n--\n--   cargo run -p hol_cli -- --course courses/proofs-checked.json --section ch02 docs/book/code/ch02-fallacies.ctea\n\nmode classical\n\n-- Fallacy 1: affirming the consequent.\n-- \"If it rained, the grass is wet. The grass is wet. So it rained.\"\ntheorem affirming_the_consequent (P Q : Prop) : (P -> Q) -> Q -> P := by\n  intro hpq\n  intro hq\n  exact hq\n\n-- Fallacy 2: denying the antecedent.\n-- \"If it rained, the grass is wet. It didn't rain. So the grass is dry.\"\ntheorem denying_the_antecedent (P Q : Prop) : (P -> Q) -> not P -> not Q := by\n  intro hpq\n  intro hnp\n  intro hq\n  apply hnp\n  exact hq\n\n-- Fallacy 3: reading \"or\" as \"and\".\n-- \"You may have soup or salad. So you may have soup.\"\ntheorem or_gives_both (P Q : Prop) : P \\/ Q -> P := by\n  intro h\n  cases h with\n  | left hp =>\n      exact hp\n  | right hq =>\n      exact hq\n\n-- Not a fallacy — a wrong turn. The statement is fine, but `left`\n-- commits to proving P, which our assumption cannot deliver. Compare\n-- the checker's note here with the notes above.\ntheorem wrong_turn (P Q : Prop) : Q -> P \\/ Q := by\n  intro hq\n  left\n  exact hq\n"
