    "name": "ch08-exercises.ctea",
    "source": "-- Proofs, Checked — Chapter 8: exercises.\n-- Replace each `sorry` with a real proof.\n--\n--   cargo run -p hol_cli -- --course courses/proofs-checked.json --section ch08 docs/book/code/ch08-exercises.ctea\n\nimport ../../../std/prelude.ctea\n\nmode constructive\n\nsort Person\n\nconst alice : Person\n\n-- Exercise 8.1 (warm-up). The squaring function's graph is total:\n-- every input has an output you can name.\ntheorem ex8_1 : Total(fun x y : Nat => x * x = y) := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 8.2. And it is single-valued — two claimed outputs for the\n-- same input must be equal. (Chapter 5 rewriting does the whole job.)\ntheorem ex8_2 : SingleValued(fun x y : Nat => x * x = y) := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 8.3. Adding two is injective. Hint: simp at h1 computes\n-- x1 + 2 into successors; then peel with succ_inj, twice.\ntheorem ex8_3 : Injective(fun x y : Nat => x + 2 = y) := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 8.4. The identity function is surjective: every y is hit —\n-- by whom?\ntheorem ex8_4 : Surjective(fun x y : Nat => x = y) := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 8.5. The constant function sending everyone to alice is\n-- total...\ntheorem ex8_5 : Total(fun x y : Person => y = alice) := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 8.6. ...and single-valued: both outputs equal alice, so\n-- they equal each other. (It is spectacularly NOT injective, but\n-- proving that would need two provably distinct Persons.)\ntheorem ex8_6 : SingleValued(fun x y : Person => y = alice) := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 8.7. Composition preserves surjectivity. Mirror the\n-- compose_preserves_injective example, with compose_surjective.\ntheorem ex8_7\n  (F : Person -> Person -> Prop)\n  (G : Person -> Person -> Prop)\n  : Surjective(F)\n    -> Surjective(G)\n    -> forall z : Person, exists x : Person, exists y : Person, F(x, y) /\\ G(y, z) := by\n  sorry\n"
