    "source": "-- Proofs, Checked — Chapter 9: common mistakes.\n-- INTENDED TO FAIL. Each theorem contains a deliberate mistake\n-- discussed in the \"Common mistakes\" section of the chapter.\n--\n--   cargo run -p hol_cli -- --course courses/proofs-checked.json --section ch09 docs/book/code/ch09-mistakes.ctea\n\nimport ../../../std/prelude.ctea\n\nmode constructive\n\n-- Mistake 1: hoping computation alone proves a claim about *every* n.\n-- With variables in the way, n + m and m + n are stuck terms:\n-- nothing computes, and refl sees two different expressions.\ntheorem comm_by_computation (n m : Nat) : n + m = m + n := by\n  refl\n\n-- Mistake 2: using the induction hypothesis as if it already proved\n-- the whole theorem. It proves the statement for k -- one rung, not\n-- the ladder. (Commutativity again, this time of multiplication.)\ntheorem ih_is_not_the_theorem (n m : Nat) : n * m = m * n := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n      simp\n      refl\n  | succ k ih =>\n      exact ih\n\n-- Mistake 3: reusing the theorem's own variable as an induction\n-- binder. The succ arm needs a *fresh* name for the predecessor.\ntheorem shadowed_binder (n : Nat) : n - n = 0 := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n      refl\n  | succ n ih =>\n      simp\n      exact ih\n\n-- Mistake 4: introducing a hypothesis about n and then inducting on n.\n-- The hypothesis talks about the original n, so the checker refuses to\n-- split n into cases underneath it.\ntheorem square_positive (n : Nat) : 1 <= n -> 1 <= n * n := by\n  intro h\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n      simp at h\n      contradiction\n  | succ k ih =>\n      simp\n      trivial\n"
