    "source": "-- Proofs, Checked — Chapter 12: exercises.\n-- Replace each `sorry` with a real proof.\n--\n--   cargo run -p hol_cli -- --course courses/proofs-checked.json --section ch12 docs/book/code/ch12-exercises.ctea\n\nimport ../../../std/prelude.ctea\n\nmode constructive\n\n-- Provided (proved in the chapter's examples file -- rebuild it here\n-- so this file stands alone): pred_le, via le_n_succ_n.\ntheorem le_n_succ_n (n : Nat) : n <= succ(n) := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n      simp\n      trivial\n  | succ k ih =>\n      simp\n      exact ih\n\ntheorem pred_le (n : Nat) : pred(n) <= n := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n      simp\n      trivial\n  | succ k ih =>\n      simp\n      exact le_n_succ_n\n\n-- Exercise 12.1 (warm-up). Practice the apply-with-a-lambda scaffolding\n-- on a statement where both goals close with `simp` then `trivial`.\n-- The point is the machinery, not the math.\ntheorem ex12_1 (n : Nat) : 0 <= n * n := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 12.2. An echo that looks back ONE step. Strong induction\n-- still works: use the strong hypothesis at k itself. What proof of\n-- k <= k does the library hand you?\nfunc echo : Nat -> Nat\n\naxiom echo_zero : echo(0) = 0\naxiom echo_step (k : Nat) : echo(succ(k)) = echo(k)\n\ntheorem ex12_2 (n : Nat) : echo(n) = 0 := by\n  sorry\n\n-- Exercise 12.3 (challenge). A siren that looks back TWO steps, like\n-- the chapter's bulbs -- but its base value is 1. Model the proof on\n-- all_bulbs_off; pred_le is provided above.\nfunc siren : Nat -> Nat\n\naxiom siren_zero : siren(0) = 1\naxiom siren_step (k : Nat) : siren(succ(k)) = siren(pred(k))\n\ntheorem ex12_3 (n : Nat) : siren(n) = 1 := by\n  sorry\n"
